course: world
lesson: 64
title: Rivers, lakes and the sea up close
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A river collects water from all the land that slopes towards it, called its drainage basin; small streams called tributaries join it on the way, so it grows bigger as it flows downhill. The line of high land between two basins is a watershed.
explain: Near its source the river is fast and steep, cutting down into the land and carrying stones; in its middle and lower course it is slower and wider, swings from side to side in loops called meanders, and spreads over flat land called a floodplain when it floods.
explain: Lakes form in hollows dug by glaciers, in craters of old volcanoes, in cracks where the crust pulled apart, or behind dams; Lake Baikal in Russia, in such a crack, is the deepest lake on Earth and holds about a fifth of all the fresh water in lakes and rivers.
explain: Only about 3 parts in 100 of all the water on Earth is fresh, and most of that is frozen in ice or hidden underground; the rest is salty ocean water. The sea is never still: waves are made by the wind, and tides, which raise and lower the sea twice a day, are made mostly by the pull of the Moon.
explain: Wetlands such as marshes and swamps soak up flood water like a sponge, clean the water, and are home to many birds, frogs and fish.
fact: A tributary is a smaller river or stream that flows into a bigger river.
fact: A drainage basin is all the land whose water drains into one river.
fact: A meander is a loop or bend in a river in its lower course.
fact: A floodplain is the flat land beside a river that is covered with water when the river floods.
fact: The Nile and the Amazon are the longest rivers in the world, and the Amazon carries by far the most water.
fact: Lake Baikal is the deepest lake on Earth.
fact: About 97 parts in 100 of the Earth's water is salty sea water.
fact: Most of the Earth's fresh water is frozen in ice sheets and glaciers or lies underground.
fact: Waves on the sea are mostly made by the wind blowing over the water.
fact: Tides are caused mostly by the pull of the Moon's gravity on the oceans.
fact: The Dead Sea is a salty lake so salty that people float in it easily.
fact: Wetlands soak up flood water and clean it.
exercise: What is a tributary? | A smaller river that flows into a bigger one | The place where a river reaches the sea and spreads out | A lake at the start of a river | A bridge over a wide river
exercise: What is a drainage basin? | All the land whose water drains into one river | The deepest part of a river's bed, where the water is still | A bowl used by scientists to measure river water | A lake at the end of a river, where the water collects
exercise: What is a meander? | A loop or bend in a river | A waterfall in the mountains | A small island in the sea | A dam built by beavers
exercise: What is a floodplain? | Flat land beside a river covered when it floods | A plane that flies over floods | A high hill above a river that never gets wet in floods | A deep lake behind a dam
exercise: Why is a river fast near its source? | The land there is steep | The water there is warmer | The river there is widest | The sea pulls the water there
exercise: Which is the deepest lake on Earth? | Lake Baikal | Lake Victoria | Lake Superior | The Caspian Sea
exercise: About how much of the Earth's water is fresh? | About 3 parts in 100 | About half of all water | About 9 parts in every 10 | Almost none of it at all
exercise: Where is most of the Earth's fresh water? | Frozen in ice or under the ground | In rivers and lakes | In clouds floating high in the sky | In the bodies of animals
exercise: What makes most waves on the sea? | The wind | The Moon | Fish swimming | The Earth's spinning
exercise: What causes the tides? | Mostly the pull of the Moon's gravity | The wind always blowing in towards the land | Rivers pouring huge amounts of water into the sea | Ships and boats moving the water around
exercise: How often does the sea rise and fall with the tides in most places? | About twice a day | Once a month | Once a year | Every few minutes
exercise: Why do people float easily in the Dead Sea? | Very salty water holds things up more strongly | The water is very warm, so it lifts you up | The water there has no waves | The bottom is shallow everywhere
exercise: Why are wetlands important? | They soak up flood water, clean it and shelter wildlife | They make the land dry and easy to build on | They make the sea saltier | They stop rivers from flowing
exercise: Which river carries the most water? | The Amazon | The Thames | The Danube | The Dnipro
test: A town is built on the flat land right beside a big slow river. What danger should it plan for? | Floods when the river overflows onto its floodplain | Lava from the riverbed | The river running uphill | Sand dunes moving in from the river banks in the dry season
test: Rain falls on two sides of a long mountain ridge. On one side it ends up in one river, on the other side in a different river. What is the ridge called? | A watershed | A meander | A tributary | A floodplain
test: Which is true about the Earth's water? | Nearly all of it is salty, and little of the fresh water is easy to reach | Most of it is fresh water in rivers | Lakes hold more water than the oceans | Fresh and salt water are about equal
test: A fisherman notices that the sea comes high up the beach twice each day. What is mainly responsible? | The Moon's gravity pulling on the ocean | The wind changing direction twice every day | The rivers emptying their water into the sea | The fish moving about the sea in large groups
test: A river runs through steep mountains and later across a wide flat plain. Where will it most likely make big loops? | On the wide flat plain | In the steep mountains | Right at its source | Only where it is fastest
test: A city drains its marshes to build houses. What might happen next? | Floods become worse because nothing soaks up the water | The river stops flowing completely and dries up into a muddy ditch | The sea becomes fresh | More birds come to live there
test: Why does a big river keep growing as it flows towards the sea? | Tributaries keep joining it | It melts the rocks of its bed | The sea pushes water into it | Fish add water as they swim
test: What would happen to waves on a lake if there were no wind at all for a long time? | The surface would become very calm | The waves would grow bigger | The lake would freeze | The water would turn salty
test: why can u float so easy in the dead sea | The water is so salty that it pushes you up much more | The water is so warm that it lifts you up | The sea is so shallow that you touch the bottom | The waves there are strong enough to hold you up
test: whats a tributary again | A smaller river or stream that flows into a bigger one | The wide mouth where a river finally meets the open sea | A loop or bend in a slow lowland river | The flat land beside a river that floods
test: my cousin says most of the earth's water is fresh water we can drink. true? | No, about 97 parts in 100 are salty sea water | Yes, most water on Earth is in rivers and lakes | Yes, rain makes most of the water on Earth fresh | No, most of it is frozen in the clouds
test: teacher said tides come from the wind blowing the sea, i think he slipped? | Yes, tides come mostly from the pull of the Moon's gravity | No, the wind is what makes the tides go in and out | No, tides come from rivers pouring into the sea | Yes, tides come from the Earth's hot core heating the sea
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a tributary? | A smaller river or stream flowing into a larger river | A loop or bend in the lower course of a river | The flat land covered by a river in flood | The point at which a river enters the sea
exam: Ascertain the proportion of the Earth's water which is salty sea water. | About 97 parts in 100 | About 50 parts in 100 | About 3 parts in 100 | About 75 parts in 100
exam: Identify the principal cause of the tides upon the Earth's oceans. | The pull of the Moon's gravity upon the oceans | The wind blowing over the surface of the sea | The flow of rivers into the oceans | Earthquakes occurring beneath the sea
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the location of the greater part of the Earth's fresh water. | It is frozen in ice or lies underground | It flows in rivers and streams | It is contained in lakes such as Baikal | It is suspended as clouds in the sky
